      "textEn": "415\n\nYairiyam Bubuje Brahmandaitirendrairanivartibhi. Of them, the first, the earth, the sky, the world, and the glory that is attained on earth. Meaning of the word\n\nYaish—by whom; Iyam—this world; Bubhuje— enjoyed; Brahman— O best of Brahmins; Daitya-Indrah— born in the clan of demons;\nBy the brave and kings; Anivartibhih— by them who were determined to die fighting or to be victorious; Tesham— of such\npersons; Kalah— by time; Agrasit— took away; Lokan— all the wealth, all the things to enjoy; Na— not; Yashah—\nPraise; Adhigataam— obtained; Bhuvi— in this world. Oh, Brahmins! Undoubtedly, those great demon kings have enjoyed this world, who never hesitated to wage war, but in due course, besides their fame, they had\neverything snatched away, and they are still present today on the strength of that very fame. In other words,\nMan should try to earn fame by leaving everything behind. Meaning: In this context, Chanakya Pandit also says.\n(Chanakya Shloka 34)—Life is momentary.\nEven one is not attainable by crores of gold. The age of a human is very short, but if someone in this short age\ndoes something that increases his fame, then he can live for millions of years. Therefore, Bali Maharaj decided that he would not follow his guru's order that Vamandev\nshould not be disturbed by the promise given. On the contrary, they decided that according to their promise,\nthey would give land, so that their count would always be included among the twelve chiefs (Balirvayasikvayam). Sulabha, Yudhi, Viprarsha, Hanyavrittastanutyaajah. And those who have come to pilgrimage with faith, these are the ones who are not worthy of being abandoned. 9. Meaning of the word:\n\nSuch are those who are easily attainable; Yudhi—in the battlefield; Vipra-rishi—O best of Brahmins; Hi—indeed; Avritta—without fear of fighting;\nTanu-tyajah—in this way, one sacrifices one's life; Na—not; Tatha—in the same way; Tirthayaa-aayate—upon the arrival of saintly men;\nFrom whom pilgrimage sites are made; Shraddhaya—with faith and devotion; Ye—those; Dhan-tyajah—one who can renounce accumulated wealth.\n. There are many people who, without fearing war, have sacrificed their lives in the battlefield.\nBut such an opportunity has rarely been given to anyone when a person has given his accumulated wealth to such a saintly man with devotion, who gives birth to a pilgrimage site. Meaning: Many Kshatriyas have sacrificed their lives for their countries, but such a\nperson will rarely be found who donates his accumulated wealth and all his property to a worthy recipient"
