      "textEn": "422\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nYajamana: one who worships (Bali Maharaj); Svayam: self; Tasya: of Vamanadeva; Shrimat: most excellent; Pada-yugam: lotus feet; Mudaa: with joy; Avanijya: washed properly; Avahat: offered; Murdhna: on the head; Tat: that; Apah: water; Vishwa-pavaniha: the one who liberates the entire world. Bali Maharaj, who worshipped Vamandev, happily washed the lotus feet of the Lord.\nThen he placed that water on his head because that water redeems the entire world. Tadasurendram Divi Devatagana\nGandharvavidhadharasiddharnah.\nAll actions are praised, even the most humble ones,\nLike the showers of the sun, the cows are fed. 19. Meaning of the word\n\nTada—At that time; Asura-Indram—To King Bali Maharaja of the Asuras; Divi—In heaven; Devata-ganah—The group of gods;\nGandharva—The Gandharvas; Vidyadhara—The Vidyadharas; Siddha—The inhabitants of Siddha Loka; Charana—The inhabitants of Charana Loka; Tat—That; Karma—\nWork; Sabre—All of them; Gruntantah—While declaring; Arjavam—Simple; Prasun-varshayah—By the rain of flowers; Vavrukshah—Of the rain; Muda-anvitah—Being very pleased with him. At that time, the residents of Swargaloka—such as gods, Gandharvas, Vidyadharas, Siddhas, and Charanas—all—were greatly pleased by this simple, non-dual work of Bali Maharaj, and they praised his virtues\nand showered him with millions of flowers. Meaning: Arjavam, meaning simplicity or being free from duality, is a quality of a Brahmin and a Vaishnava.\nA Vaishnava\nAttains all the qualities of a Brahmin—\n\nYasyasti bhakti bhagavatyakincha\n\nSarvaairgunaiastatra samasate surah\n\n( Bhagavat 5.1.12 )\n\nA Vaishnava should have all the qualities of a Brahmin—namely, truth, self-control, endurance, and humility. Vaishnava.\nThere can be no hypocrisy in their conduct. When Bali Maharaj, with unwavering faith and devotion,\nserved the lotus feet of Lord Vishnu, all the inhabitants of Swargaloka praised this act with utmost\nappreciation."
